{
  "_comment": "FormalShell pywal template. Not run by the shell — pywal renders it, you wire the output up yourself: drop this file at ~/.config/wal/templates/pywal-theme.json, run `wal -i <image>`, pywal writes the rendered copy to ~/.cache/wal/pywal-theme.json, then symlink (or a `wal -o` post-run hook copies) that file to $XDG_STATE_HOME/formalshell/theme.json (usually ~/.local/state/formalshell/theme.json) — FormalShell's Theme.qml watches that path and re-themes live on change. Pywal's 16-color ANSI palette can't express all four ink bands as distinctly as matugen's Material roles (theme.json.tmpl); this mapping follows the common pywal convention (color8 as the muted/comment tone, color4 as accent, color1/color3 as urgent/warning) and reuses color0 for both backgroundAlt and rule since both need to sit close to background. Pywal has no light/dark template variable, so mode below is hardcoded dark — hand-edit it to \"light\" after generating with `wal -l`. This file must stay valid JSON after pywal's substitution — this comment is a JSON field for that reason, not a // line.",
  "mode": "dark",
  "background": "{background}",
  "backgroundAlt": "{color0}",
  "foreground": "{foreground}",
  "foregroundDim": "{color7}",
  "foregroundFaint": "{color8}",
  "accent": "{color4}",
  "urgent": "{color1}",
  "warning": "{color3}",
  "rule": "{color0}",
  "onAccent": "{background}",
  "onUrgent": "{background}",
  "onWarning": "{background}"
}
